**(i) Hacking (2 marks).** Hacking is gaining unauthorised access to a computer system, network or data (without the permission of the owner), usually by getting round its security.

**(ii) Three measures to prevent hacking (3 marks, 1 mark each).** Any three of:

- use strong passwords and change them regularly (or use two-factor or biometric authentication);
- install and update a firewall;
- encrypt sensitive data and network traffic;
- install and update anti-virus and anti-malware software, and update the operating system and applications;
- give users access rights only to the files they need;
- keep computers in locked rooms and log off when leaving.

**(iii) Three problems caused by unethical hacking (3 marks, 1 mark each).** Any three of:

- theft of data or of money (identity theft, fraud);
- damage, corruption or deletion of data and programs, including the spreading of viruses;
- loss of privacy and of confidential information;
- the system is slowed down or put out of service, which stops the work of the organisation;
- loss of reputation and of customers' trust, and legal penalties.
